            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has an excess amount of air leakage (heat loss) that should be addressed. The current air leakage locations include:



** The lower heated space below the main floor had a fair bit of air coming up during the blower door test. There was a fair bit of stuff located below the hatch so it was challenging to see the exact locations, however key areas you should check include: the rear access panel, the utilidor connection, around any pipes &amp; wires that transition out the side walls, the perimeter header space and any holes around pipes that transition up through the main floor and into the attic (ie: plumbing stacks).



** The older windows have broken closure hardware and do not seal well. The rear door would not close well and had leakage along the bottom and the left "opening" side.



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The chimney for the current boiler



** The wall cavity above the bathroom wall was becoming quite cold during the blower door test, that indicates there are openings into the attic (perhaps around wires or the adjoining section of the front/back halves of your house). If you decide to upgrade the bathroom fan and/or add more attic insulation - this would be a good time to look into this further ........ and fill the holes with spray foam



Savings: I modeled a reasonable 20% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 313 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.8 GJ.



There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate; achieving a 20% reduction increases the rebate to $750, and a 30%+ reduction achieves the maximum rebate of $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your main house are 2x4 (R12) and you mentioned there was an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of exterior insulation that was noticed when 2 of the front windows were replaced. The rear addition walls are 2x6 (R20). You were interested in knowing the estimated energy savings and rebates that you may be eligible for if you added more exterior wall insulation. We discussed an option for you to add another 3" (R15) of extruded polystyrene on the exterior - bringing the total wall nominal R-value up to about R35 ....... which is what would be required for newer house construction.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding another R15.0, as outlined above.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 800 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic space of the original house apprears to have the original 7" (R28) of fibreglass insulation. The rear addition is a cathedral ceiling that I have assumed also has R28. The roof of the original house is a low slope design and there really isn't alot of room to add more insulation, without blocking off the ventilation of this attic space - I will leave this up to you to review with a local contractor.



For houses that currently have &gt;R24 attic insulation, there is only one rebate available - $125 if you increase up to a minimum R50. If you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade under the DHERP, this rebate would be double to $250.



Savings:

By increasing attic insulation of the original house from R28 up to R50, you could potentially save $ 313 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 250 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.



Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

You house has 10 windows and you have recently upgraded 2 of the front windows to new ENERGY STAR?????? units. If you proceed with a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, this would be a good time to upgrade the remaining 8 windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By upgrading the remaining 8 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 429 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.3 GJ, and qualify for a $ 250 rebate per window (8 windows = $2,000) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system so please see the previous "Heating System" section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 85.3% efficient oil boiler that used to previously also heat the domestic hot water for your house. That "interconnection" has been removed so the hot water in your house is provided by the electric hot water tank. The current boiler vents up a 6" chimney (that allows air leakage heat loss even when the boiler is not operating) and there is a 5" combustion air supply pipe up through the floor (that was stuffed with plastic bags when I was there).



As you have a gas service right to your house, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to a 95% AFUE combination gas boiler that will both heat your house &amp; provide your hot water. This new boiler would eliminate the need for the current 6" chimney and the 5" combustion air supply pipe ....... as the new boiler would be direct vent out the side wall of the rear addition via 2 PVC pipes. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage), this is an ideal time to consider a new heating &amp; hot water system.



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler &amp; electric hot water tank with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing "combi" gas boiler, you could potentially save $ 3,166 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 46.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,250   from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $12,500 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $5,000 for the wall insulation

     - $   250 for the ceiling insulation

     - $2,000 for the windows

     - $2,500 for the heating system

     - $   750 for a 20% air leakage reduction ($500 or $1,000 if you achieve a 10% or 30% reduction)

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate and the ceiling insulation, however these rebates would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR washing machine - $200

     - ESTAR refrigerator - $200

     - ESTAR dishwasher - 50% up to $200

     - ECM circulation pumps for your new "combi" boiler - 50% up to $175 each (up to 5 per home)

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (just the clothes dryer as both the range hood &amp; bathroom fan vent to the inside of the house), indicated a pressure drop of 1.5 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current boiler is chimney vented and can be susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there was both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has very little exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - The bathroom fan vents into the lower level &amp; not to the outside

        - The clothes dryer does vent to the outside



As discussed, you will want to have the bathroom fan vented to the outside of the house &amp; this is a good time to update the fan to a high efficiency Energy Star rated unit, that uses very little electricity, operates at low noise (sone) levels, and will have a good internal damper to seal &amp; prevent excess air leakage heat loss. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Inuvik



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.73 per kWh thereafter

Synthetic Natural Gas (Utilidor Piped) - $37.21/Gigajoule

Oil  - $1.46 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $12.60 per bag, $630.00 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



